---
import ContentPage from "@/layouts/ContentPage.astro";
import DocSection from "@/components/DocSection.astro";
import { APP_NAME, SUPPORT_EMAIL } from "@/lib/appConfig";

export const prerender = true;

// Bump when the policy's substance changes; "Changes to this policy" promises it.
const EFFECTIVE = "November 10, 2026";
---

{
  /* Unlisted like /about: linked from the footer and from App Store Connect,
    kept out of search results. public/robots.txt disallows the fetch. Every
    claim below is about what the code does today — check the data flows
    (apps/mobile, apps/site/src/pages/api) before changing one. */
}
<ContentPage
  title={`Privacy Policy — ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${APP_NAME} collects, why, who receives it, and how to delete it.`}
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  noindex
>
  <p class="text-muted mt-4 text-sm">Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>

  <div class="text-muted mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed">
    <p>
      I'm James Mitofsky, and I run {APP_NAME}: the {APP_NAME} app for iPhone and the website at{
        " "
      }
      <a href="https://waterrun.app" class="underline underline-offset-4">waterrun.app</a>. This
      policy explains what information {APP_NAME} handles, how it gets it, what it's used for, who else
      receives it, how long it's kept, and how you can withdraw your consent or have it deleted.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      The short version: {APP_NAME} has no accounts of its own, no ads, no analytics and no tracking.
      Your location is used only to find fountains, guide your runs and place the fountains you add. I
      never sell your information.
    </p>
  </div>

  <DocSection heading={`What ${APP_NAME} collects`}>
    <h3>Your location</h3>
    <p>
      The app uses your device's precise location to show where you are on the map, find fountains
      near you, plan a route from where you're standing, and guide you to each fountain. It asks for
      permission first. During a run it keeps using your location while the screen is locked, so
      guidance and your progress keep going; that stops when the run ends. The app doesn't use your
      location in the background at any other time.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find fountains and plan routes, the app sends {APP_NAME}'s server the area you're searching
      and the stops on your route, which usually begin at your location. The server uses them to
      answer that request and doesn't store them. If you add a new fountain, its position (usually
      where you're standing) is published to the map with the fountain.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the website, the map's locate button asks your browser for your location to centre the map
      on you. The site then asks its server for the fountains in the area on screen.
    </p>

    <h3>Your OpenStreetMap sign-in</h3>
    <p>
      {APP_NAME}'s fountain data lives on OpenStreetMap, the free public map anyone can use and
      edit. To send fountain updates you sign in with an OpenStreetMap account, on openstreetmap.org
      itself, so {APP_NAME} never sees your password. Signing in gives {APP_NAME} a key that lets it read
      your account details and send map edits on your behalf. Of those details, {APP_NAME} uses only your
      username, profile picture, number of contributions and the date you joined, to show them in the
      app. The key gives no access to your email address or your messages.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the app, that key is kept in your device's secure keychain. On the website, it's kept in a
      secure cookie that page scripts can't read. With each request, {APP_NAME}'s server passes the
      key on to OpenStreetMap; it doesn't store the key or your account details.
    </p>

    <h3>The fountain updates you send</h3>
    <p>
      When you report a fountain as working, partially working, out of order or removed, describe it
      (who it serves, how it dispenses water, whether it's seasonal), write a note on it, or add a
      new fountain, that update is published to OpenStreetMap under your OpenStreetMap account, with
      the time and a mark saying it was made with {APP_NAME}. <strong>Updates are public:</strong> anyone
      can see them and which account made them, and they stay in the map's public edit history. Please
      don't put personal information in notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      {APP_NAME}'s server keeps two small records of each update it sends: a log entry saying which
      fountain changed, how, and the edit's public reference number; and a copy of the updated
      fountain for about a day, so the change shows on {APP_NAME}'s map before the public map data
      catches up.
    </p>

    <h3>What stays on your phone</h3>
    <p>
      Your run history (each run's date, start point, planned route, stops and what you found at
      each), the route you're planning, and any updates waiting to be sent are stored only on your
      device. They're never uploaded to me. Waiting updates go to OpenStreetMap once you're signed
      in and online, as described above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run alerts (a fountain coming up, a run finishing, your progress while the screen is locked)
      and the run's Live Activity are created on your device. {APP_NAME} doesn't use push notifications,
      so it never collects a device token.
    </p>

    <h3>What you send through the website's forms</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The waitlist form collects your name and email address.</li>
      <li>The feedback form collects your message and, only if you give it, your email address.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both are delivered to me through Formspree, a form service.</p>

    <h3>Routine server records and cookies</h3>
    <p>
      Like any online service, the servers {APP_NAME} runs on (hosted by Vercel) record routine details
      of each request: IP address, browser or app version, the address requested and the time. These records
      are used only to keep the service running and secure.
    </p>
    <p>
      The website sets cookies only for signing in: short-lived ones (ten minutes) that complete a
      sign-in securely, and the sign-in cookie described above, which lasts up to seven days. It
      uses no advertising or analytics cookies.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading={`What ${APP_NAME} doesn't collect`}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No analytics, crash reporting or advertising tools, in the app or on the website, and no
        tracking across other companies' apps and websites.
      </li>
      <li>
        No health or fitness data: {APP_NAME} doesn't read from or write to Apple Health, and it doesn't
        record your pace or heart rate.
      </li>
      <li>No contacts, photos, camera, microphone or files.</li>
      <li>
        Nothing is sold, rented or shared for advertising or marketing, and your location is never
        used for advertising or marketing.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="How it's used">
    <p>{APP_NAME} uses the information above only to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>show you the map and the fountains around you;</li>
      <li>plan your routes and guide you during a run;</li>
      <li>publish the fountain updates you choose to send, and show your profile in the app;</li>
      <li>reply to your feedback and send you a waitlist invitation;</li>
      <li>keep the service running, secure and free of abuse.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It isn't used for anything else: not for advertising, not for profiling, and not for sale.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Who else receives it">
    <p>
      {APP_NAME} relies on a handful of services, and each receives only what it needs for its part:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenStreetMap</strong> (the OpenStreetMap Foundation) handles your sign-in, provides your
        account details, and publishes the updates you send. In the app, your device also loads map images
        straight from OpenStreetMap's map servers, which see your IP address and the part of the map on
        screen. See the{" "}
        <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy">OpenStreetMap privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public map data search servers</strong> (the volunteer-run{" "}
        <a href="https://overpass-api.de">Overpass API</a> servers) receive the map areas being searched
        for fountains. They hear from {APP_NAME}'s server, not from your device, so they don't
        receive your IP address or who you are.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="https://brouter.de">BRouter</a></strong>, a volunteer-run route planner,
        receives the stops of a route being planned, so it can work out the path on foot between
        them. It also hears only from {APP_NAME}'s server.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="https://openfreemap.org">OpenFreeMap</a></strong> supplies the website map's
        images, and <strong><a href="https://maplibre.org">MapLibre</a></strong> the lettering on the
        app's map. Your browser or device loads them directly, so these services see your IP address and
        the part of the map on screen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vercel</strong> hosts the website and {APP_NAME}'s server, and keeps the routine
        records described above. See <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy"
          >Vercel's privacy policy</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Formspree</strong> receives and delivers what you send through the website's forms. See{
          " "
        }
        <a href="https://formspree.io/legal/privacy-policy">Formspree's privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I share information only with services that protect it at least as well as this policy does,
      and only so they can provide their part of {APP_NAME}. I don't share it with anyone else,
      unless the law requires me to.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="How long it's kept">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>On your phone:</strong> run history and your planned route stay until you delete the app.
        Waiting updates stay until they're sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your sign-in:</strong> in the app, until you sign out; on the website, until you sign
        out or after seven days, whichever comes first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Locations sent to find fountains or plan routes:</strong> not kept once the request has
        been answered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The server's records of your updates:</strong> the copy of an updated fountain is deleted
        after about a day. Log entries and routine server records are deleted automatically by Vercel
        after a limited period; I don't copy them anywhere else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Published updates:</strong> they're part of OpenStreetMap's public map and its history,
        which OpenStreetMap keeps under its own policy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Waitlist sign-ups:</strong> until you've been invited and no longer want updates, or until
        you ask me to delete yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feedback:</strong> for as long as it helps me follow up and improve {APP_NAME}, or
        until you ask me to delete it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Your choices" id="your-choices">
    <p>You can withdraw your consent or remove your information at any time:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Location:</strong> change or turn off {APP_NAME}'s access in Settings › {APP_NAME} › Location.
        Features that need your location, like run guidance, won't work without it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notifications:</strong> turn them off in Settings › {APP_NAME} › Notifications.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Signing out:</strong> in the app, go to Profile › Sign Out. This removes the sign-in key
        from your device. To cut off {APP_NAME}'s access entirely, revoke it in your{" "}
        <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/oauth2/authorized_applications"
          >OpenStreetMap account settings</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Data on your phone:</strong> deleting the app deletes your run history, your planned route
        and any updates that haven't been sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your account:</strong>{" "}
        {APP_NAME} doesn't create or keep an account for you, so there's no {APP_NAME} account to delete.
        Your OpenStreetMap account is OpenStreetMap's: you can{" "}
        <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/account/deletion">delete it there</a>. Updates you've
        already published stay part of the map's public history, as the{" "}
        <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy">OpenStreetMap privacy policy</a> explains.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Everything else:</strong> to see, correct or delete your waitlist sign-up, your feedback,
        or anything else I hold about you, email me at <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}
          >{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a
        >. I'll reply within 30 days.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Children">
    <p>
      {APP_NAME} isn't directed at children under 13, and I don't knowingly collect information from them.
      If you believe a child has sent me information, email me and I'll delete it.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Security">
    <p>
      Everything {APP_NAME} sends travels over encrypted connections, and the sign-in key is kept in your
      device's keychain or a cookie scripts can't read. Still, no way of sending or storing information
      over the internet is completely secure, so while I work to protect your information I can't guarantee
      its absolute security.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Changes to this policy">
    <p>
      This policy is effective as of {EFFECTIVE}. If I change it, I'll post the new version on this
      page with a new effective date, and for a significant change I'll also put a notice on the
      website. Please check back from time to time.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection heading="Contact">
    <p>
      For any questions or concerns about this policy or your information, email me at{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>
  </DocSection>
</ContentPage>
